#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069612} :activity/id #uuid "b16543e4-9398-58b9-b3c3-d37c4e46b486" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050771 :topic/id #uuid "655ce7e8-5c0a-5ddc-a7e7-f1d2126f4eba" :topic/math-academy-id 443} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070014 :tutorial/content "How can we solve an integral like\n\n$$\n\\int 2x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x \\,?\n$$\n\nNotice that if we let $u=x^2+1$, then the derivative is $u' = 2x,$ and both $u$ and $u'$ are present in the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\int \\underbrace{2x}_{u'}(\\overbrace{x^2+1}^{u})^3\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIntegrals like this can always be solved using substitution. So, let's proceed. If we let ${\\color{red}{u=x^2+1}},$ then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\color{blue}\\textrm{d}u=2x \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe can now see that the change of variables from $x$ to $u$ is convenient because if we substitute, we get\n\n$$\n\\int {\\color{blue}{2x}}({\\color{red}{x^2+1}})^3\\,{\\color{blue}{\\textrm{d}x}} =  \\int \\color{red}(x^2+1)^{\\color{black}3}\\, \\color{blue}{2x\\,\\textrm{d}x} \\color{black}= \\int {\\color{red}u}^{\\color{black}3}\\, {\\color{blue}{\\,\\textrm{d}u}} .\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the integral on the right since it's the integral of a power function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{3}\\,\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the last step, we change the variable back to $x$ by replacing $u=x^2+1.$ So the final result is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int 2x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x \n=\\dfrac{(x^2+1)^4}{4}+C.\n$$\n\n**Note:** After solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)^{4}}{4}+C] & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[({x}^{2}+1)^{4}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4({x}^{2}+1)^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+1)+0 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{3}\\cdot (2x) \\\\  & =2x({x}^{2}+1)^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4b1e3de2-02c8-5c3b-8e85-606d3fb2a801" :tutorial/math-academy-id 835 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "122952df-ade4-5fd8-a8d0-4755ccb3a2b4" :step/math-academy-id 1608 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069613}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068722 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068564 :question/id #uuid "e2579380-1933-5819-a39e-9ffcfdafeeee" :question/math-academy-id "e-452"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b89c50f3-2181-5b61-96cb-386ef88d2035" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050628}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339100}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Expression Involving a Power Function Multiplied by Its Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "051164fd-284e-552d-9f28-b9c59e3a66be" :step/math-academy-id 1609 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069614}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068723 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068565 :question/id #uuid "9bf7d343-8d3d-5194-93c4-7b80bc1e3609" :question/math-academy-id "e-453"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fe2a279-0b92-561e-b17d-b902f43725ac" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339123}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating an Expression Involving a Power Function Multiplied by a Constant Multiple of Its Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "b81b95bc-62ed-53b5-89a4-fa5c9f0b6ce1" :step/math-academy-id 1612 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069615}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069615 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070015 :tutorial/content "How do we calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{2x}{1+x^2}\\,\\textrm d x?$\n\nIn this case, the numerator is the derivative of the denominator! Again, integrals like this are always solvable by substitution. So, we let\n\n$$\n\\color{red}u = x^2+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad {\\color{blue}{\\textrm d u = 2x \\, \\textrm d x}},\n$$\n\nand we can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\cdot 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve the integral in terms of $u$ and then write the final result in terms of $x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\operatorname{ln}|u|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}|1+{x}^{2}|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}(1+{x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int \\dfrac{2x}{1+x^2}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\ln(1+x^2)+C.\n$$\n\nNotice that we removed the absolute value bars because the function $1+x^2$ is always positive." :tutorial/id #uuid "f4d80988-fa2c-534a-9e1a-653904508b5e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 838 :tutorial/title "Integrating Rational Functions Using Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "b89e3ba3-9d7e-5f4c-af25-daf3385dfd27" :step/math-academy-id 1615 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069616}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068724 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068566 :question/id #uuid "49f679c4-bbc4-59a5-9b0c-0f3b69d7cf57" :question/math-academy-id "e-454"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "18ce1807-3a09-5ebd-b3cd-a91db8d2b4a8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048557}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341840}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function Using Natural Logarithms"} :step/id #uuid "f947b337-8a7f-55c4-847d-ab36ef91ad40" :step/math-academy-id 1616 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069706}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069706 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068786 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068628 :question/id #uuid "0461f8ba-c3c5-5e22-a679-32ee4f2a01b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-6149"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03e934c8-da65-5615-9717-a50eee3577fd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332756}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Other Fractional Expressions"} :step/id #uuid "8fd95143-4254-533f-8d05-73f6865604aa" :step/math-academy-id 22929}] :activity/title "Integration Using Substitution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068971}]}
